title: The crowd is thickened,
new-page: true

It was slow moving, cloth and flesh, you could see it more clearly now, maybe two hundred of you? Not many, less than you had hoped.

Still, the gaiety remained, a stubborn bloom, a bit of tension remained in the air though.

“Hey!” Shouted a man, you snapped forwards, there was a man with a pushcart, you checked it, he was selling pretzels, the salt was glittering, it looked beautiful. 

“You almost hit me, watch it!” Erika glared at you meancingly, you apologised profusely “I’m so sorry, friend, here, give me two.” You said, pointing at the pretzels, reaching into your pocket without so much as a second thought. 

The man’s frown faded, replaced with a warm, gentle smile “The Lady’s is free,” He said, turning to Erika, turning back to You though he said, “But for you? This blind man,” He jested “Give me ten Pfennings.” You nodded and tossed it to the man who smiled “Now that’s white bread you see.” 

You nodded again, “Thanks.” You said calmly, the man pushed his cart onwards. 

That wasn’t the only incident that day, a group of children chased a hoop through the legs of the adults. 

Shrieks of laughter cut through the most serious chants you now realised had just began.

“Brot und Freiheit!” Erika’s voice, that gentle, sing songy voice, now roared, taking it up, clear and strong, Hannah joined. 

You mouthed the words, but you focused on your Pretzel, it was nice. You could hear some speech, mildly, from the front, there was a man on a flatbed truck ahead. 

It was kinda hard to tell but you heard him shouting, something about a mockery of the Mayday rally. You weren’t that sure, honestly, but you tightened your grip on Erika’s hand. Your thoughts were elsewhere than this, of your little flat, of your little crib you were building, made out of old packing crates, the wood shavings you noticed still clung to your work trousers. 

You were disrupted from those peaceful thoughts with a shout, a flurry of movement, a forest of fists, Erika’s amongst them, breaking from yours, you instinctively raised it, dropping your Pretzel, “Fuck” you sighed, you picked it up “Eugh.” You stuffed it into your pocket, you’d probably end up eating it later. It was a little soggy.


